Python Arrays – Quick Revision Notes

Array: A linear data structure that stores homogeneous (same data type) elements.
Memory: Elements are stored in contiguous memory locations.
Indexing: Starts from 0.
Purpose: Store and access multiple values efficiently.

Types of Arrays in Python :
List – Stores mixed data types, dynamic, most commonly used.
array module – Stores only one data type, memory-efficient.
NumPy Array – Fast numerical and multi-dimensional computations.

Creating an Array :
import array
a = array.array('i', [1, 2, 3])
'i' → Integer typecode.

Common Operations :

append(x) → Add element at the end.
insert(index, x) → Insert at a specific position.
remove(x) → Remove first occurrence.
pop(index) → Remove element by index.
a[index] → Access an element.
a[index] = value → Update an element.
index(x) → Find the index of an element.
count(x) → Count occurrences.
reverse() → Reverse the array.
extend(iterable) → Add multiple elements.
a[start:end] → Slice the array.

Advantages :
Faster index-based access (O(1)).
Memory-efficient (using the array module).
Good for storing numerical data.

When to Use :
List → General programming.
array module → Homogeneous data with better memory efficiency.
NumPy → Data Science, Machine Learning, and mathematical operations.

Interview Keywords :
✅ Homogeneous data
✅ Contiguous memory
✅ Index starts at 0
✅ O(1) access by index
✅ List vs Array vs NumPy Array
